{"name":"Phthalo Blue","hex":"#000f89","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"phthalo blue","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bright greenish-blue crystalline synthetic pigment from the group of phthalocyanine dyes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/phthalo_blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Copper phthalocyanine","blocks":[{"text":"Copper phthalocyanine (CuPc), also called phthalocyanine blue, phthalo blue and many other names, is a bright, crystalline, synthetic blue pigment from the group of dyes based on phthalocyanines. Its brilliant blue is frequently used in paints and dyes. It is highly valued for its superior properties such as light fastness, tinting strength, covering power and resistance to the effects of alkalis and acids. It has the appearance of a blue powder, insoluble in most solvents including water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Copper_phthalocyanine.svg/330px-Copper_phthalocyanine.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_phthalocyanine","wikidata":"Q282557"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG/250px-Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG/250px-Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACopper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/CUPOCY15.png/500px-CUPOCY15.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/CUPOCY15.png/500px-CUPOCY15.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portion of crystal structure of CuPc, highlighting its slipped-stack packing motif.[18]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACUPOCY15.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Phthalocyanine_AFM.jpg/330px-Phthalocyanine_AFM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Phthalocyanine_AFM.jpg/330px-Phthalocyanine_AFM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"AFM image of Fe and Co phthalocyanines","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhthalocyanine_AFM.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/2010._%D0%94%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%86%D0%BA._%D0%9A%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8C_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0_010.jpg/500px-2010._%D0%94%D0%BE